s/42a77771cd98666593703eebd649050f12995e3edc08a0077e8e10ae68415c50.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y={g}^{″}(x).$ Find the intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={g}^{″}(x)$, we can see that ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-1,0),$ and $(1,3).$ Therefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave up on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-1,0),$ and $(1,3).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-78970/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1b60735-9dae-5c23-991d-0241c208b1b7"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-78970"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-31229"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/3891159e9d53769e4f95176a2585e339ca347e39aa91c9df8cd6456f8d3155da.png)\n\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={g}^{″}(x)$ of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. \nFind the intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave down." :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x)$ is concave down over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${g}^{″}(x)<0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y={g}^{″}(x),$ we can see that ${g}^{″}(x)<0$ on the intervals $(-7,-6)$ and $(2,5).$ \n\nTherefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave down on the intervals $(-7,-6)$ and $(2,5).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-114277/selection/c878bcdb91a0a5248ccd2809513b6e178833edd108ada4349638294858cbf7f2" :answer/id #uuid "a0ab2773-bf4c-52c2-ba6c-89081c9bdd5a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114277/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "98153d91-ae04-593a-acd3-4afb60a74acf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114277/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "a91c2336-be0d-54a9-85f2-4ccd2edc27d2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114277/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "5bbd670c-e59b-51e5-8b48-a6c89b0f0fed" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114277/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "ae1c2967-fe66-5164-8106-1c48cf85e021" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114277/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5f1e316f-78a1-538e-a241-00deb8b4f4bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-114277/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-114277/selection/c878bcdb91a0a5248ccd2809513b6e178833edd108ada4349638294858cbf7f2" "q-114277/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-114277/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-114277/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-114277/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-114277" :question/id #uuid "b0fa5633-3d2b-5f5b-a938-ef10da4c0590" :question/math-academy-id "q-114277" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ defined on $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x-\\frac{6}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6({x}^{3}-1)}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6({x}^{3}-1)}{{x}^{2}} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-1 & >0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & >1 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the fact that ${x}^{2}>0.$\n\nHence, ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ when $x>1.$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality ${x}^{3}-1<0,$ which has the solution $(0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-114277/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-114277"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-114271/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "e74db172-d46a-5ed3-82af-e90331457394" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114271/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "ee5399f1-9fff-5cb2-8ad0-4cb50b507b1f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114271/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "6a88625d-af27-53c4-a457-58e65edd2cbc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114271/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "81568835-0591-53fb-b210-bf613993fc9f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114271/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "5a252763-0942-541b-b522-67772f90d79f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114271/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fc355f65-3bf9-5528-8a74-4acdf8839e82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-114271/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-114271/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-114271/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-114271/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-114271/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-114271/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-114271" :question/id #uuid "c7ee43f3-f778-5c3e-9099-6bca7ddcc6dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-114271" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3{x}^{5}-5{x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(1,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,0)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(1,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =15{x}^{4}-20{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =60{x}^{3}-60{x}^{2} \\\\  & =60{x}^{2}(x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60{x}^{2}(x-1) & >0 \\\\ x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval   $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning, this leads to the inequality $x-1<0$ with $x\\ne 0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-114271/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-114271"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-114270/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "66e76bc3-16ac-5dbd-8410-79bf932a02c3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114270/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "2831d3e9-88ae-5a2f-8f3a-5c0526a007bd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114270/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "57a153c2-4712-5abd-aeb8-86da041cec06" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114270/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "8ffde426-a97f-5dd2-afa0-be7cd1c04dca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114270/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "03d4f9be-e445-52f2-9e85-2b5fc15af3e6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-114270/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "71107757-ea07-51d8-90b3-bf50cdd589cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-114270/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-114270/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-114270/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-114270/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-114270/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-114270/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-114270" :question/id #uuid "7af01e6f-dc86-5725-9b0b-63602f01e9af" :question/math-academy-id "q-114270" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4x+5}{{e}^{x}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-1,1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,-1)$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-({x}^{2}+4x+5){e}^{-x}+(2x+4){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+2x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =-(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(x+1)^{2}{e}^{-x}-2(x+1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x+1){e}^{-x} & >0 \\\\ (x-1)(x+1) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=(x+1)(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/09b4b237ec87a63e6de47eea5ed9156febbed0271dd0688c0be1c22a62c50c4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $(x+1)(x-1)>0$ on  $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$  So the function is concave up on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-1)$ and $(1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $(x+1)(x-1)<0,$ which has the solution $(-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-114270/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-114270"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-79045/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "63d3657e-ae5f-516e-9276-b94fb8b180c1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79045/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "5e181f05-c15f-5f8b-9d6d-ca8102d59c0c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79045/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "cb5aa965-8afa-5415-af0b-d1b2a70d19d3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79045/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "852e8b94-892d-511b-8eaa-81b95b81cc2f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79045/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "44adaf0e-0b8e-5f97-943b-400b3abc0e20" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-79045/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0e2d64a4-38f8-5c58-bd1e-7e22431bd2b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-79045/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-79045/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-79045/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-79045/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-79045/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-79045/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-79045" :question/id #uuid "8918c88a-2dbd-5d14-97dd-c946bdc87986" :question/math-academy-id "q-79045" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=6+{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(2,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(2,\\infty )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,2)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-x & >0\\,⟹\\,x<2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the function is concave up on $(-\\infty ,2).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $2-x<0,$ which has the solution $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-79045/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-79045"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-78960/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "590a86a7-7b83-5e23-b212-b72dd4a821bb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78960/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "ff08702c-faac-5647-937f-5eae3fc72903" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78960/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "ebeca85c-2e86-52b1-b789-2516911f742b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78960/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "5c28336b-f7c0-5a09-964d-ad3bdebc6b17" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78960/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "dbf0a318-778e-552c-bf70-7cffc28f8051" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-78960/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7c615065-85e9-5941-ae1b-b5ba93c5bde0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-78960/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-78960/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-78960/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-78960/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-78960/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-78960/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-78960" :question/id #uuid "bf81ab16-a4e9-51ca-976b-3909948534cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-78960" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=({x}^{2}-6)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ defined on $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-({x}^{2}-6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =(2-{x}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(2-{x}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve ${f}^{″}(x)>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & >0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ over the interval $[0,\\pi ].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/410e0af2ed20a065c27da5edaf0095dfd22c8a37a92e3d8fbaeece629cefa278.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x<0$ when $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<\\pi .$ So the graph is concave up on the interval $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x>0,$ which has the solution $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-78960/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "437c168a-6799-5d13-bd9b-031290a84da6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-78960"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3740"] :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if $f''(x)> 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b).$\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x)= x^3 -3x \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality $f''(x) > 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x & >0 \\\\ x & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(0, \\infty).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3745"] :question/problem "The graph below shows $y=g''(x),$ where the function $g(x)$ is defined on $[-5,5].$ Find the intervals on which the function $g(x)$ is concave up. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/a4d19390678a977117b32211d87df80f15e863fbe93d4068bab0a9bf1080cb0b.png)" :question/worked-solution "A function $g(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if $g''(x)> 0$ for all $x \\in (a,b).$\n\nOn the given graph of $y=g''(x),$ we can see that $g''(x) >0$ on the interval $(-1,5).$ Therefore, the function $g(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(-1,5).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3741"] :question/problem "Find the intervals of concavity for the function $f(x) = 2 x^3 - x^4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the second derivative of $f(x),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =2{x}^{3}-{x}^{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =6{x}^{2}-4{x}^{3} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12x-12{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find where the function is concave up, we need to solve $f''(x) > 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-12{x}^{2} & >0 \\\\ 12{x}^{2}-12x & <0 \\\\ x(x-1) & <0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this inequality by considering the graph of $y=x(x-1).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032645/assets/54ae61b315630925ec612500a451a90f04b19cf0f5e455c2f3db597c79cebfa1.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that $x(x-1) < 0$ when $0 < x < 1.$   So the graph is concave up on the interval  $(0,1).$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is concave down when $f''(x) <          0.$ By similar reasoning to before, this leads to the inequality $x(x-1)  >        0,$ which has the solution $(-\\infty,0)$ and $\\left(1,\\infty\\right).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
